  chat, and wait for your yes rather than writing a spec.

  One thing to settle first, because it changes the shape of the change:

  [1mThe fork:[0m [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m is the thing that [3mestablishes[0m who the user is. At the call site ([38;5;153mapp.js:19-23[39m) the
  only data available is what the form holds — username and password. There is no userId in scope to pass in, and the
  form has no field for one. So "add a userId parameter" can mean three different things, and they produce different
  code.

  - [1mClient-generated correlation ID[0m — the caller mints an ID ([38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m) and passes it in so the login attempt
    can be traced across client and server logs. Buys you: tracking of [3mattempts[0m, including failed ones. Costs: the ID
    isn't the user's real identity, so it can't be used for anything identity-related later. Cheap to change.
  - [1muserId comes back from login, not into it[0m — leave the signature alone and put the identifier in the return value ([38;5;153m{
[39m    [38;5;153msuccess, user, userId }[39m), logging it after the call. Buys you: a real identity, sourced where it's actually known.
    Costs: doesn't satisfy "add a parameter" literally, and tracks only successful logins. Cheap to change.
  - [1muserId is real caller-supplied input[0m — an account/tenant ID the user actually types, which means a new form field in
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, a [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m rule, and a new required argument. Buys you: identity known up front. Costs: changes the
    UI and the validation contract; the most expensive to undo.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the correlation ID. It matches your literal request (a new parameter on [38;5;153mlogin[39m), it's the only one
  of the three where the caller genuinely has something to pass at line 23, and it tracks failed attempts too — which is
  usually the point of login tracking. But if "who logged in" means the real account identity, the second option is the
  honest answer and the parameter is the wrong place for it.
